      "title": "Cascade vs YuNet, then SFace identification",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Two class-gated detectors and one class-gated recogniser.\n\nRow 1 - cv2.CascadeClassifier, the pre-deep-learning Viola-Jones detector: a cascade of boosted Haar stages serialized as an XML file. OpenCV 5 DELETED the data/ folder that used to ship those XMLs (cv2.data.haarcascades is an empty directory in this build), so drop them in ComfyUI/models/cascades - they are still on the opencv 4.x branch under data/haarcascades and data/lbpcascades, and they still load here.\nRow 2 - cv2.FaceDetectorYN (YuNet) on the same photo, for the accuracy comparison. Both are DATA-only nodes feeding the core 'Draw BBoxes'.\nRow 3 - recognition. YuNet says WHERE a face is; cv2.FaceRecognizerSF says WHOSE it is. 'CV SFace Embeddings' aligns each face onto a canonical 112x112 crop using ONLY the five landmarks (which is what makes the 128-D vector pose-invariant) and 'CV Embedding Match' compares the scene faces against a one-photo gallery.\n\nThe match node is deliberately pure array maths - no model file - so it works on ANY embeddings, including 'CV Deep Features' backbone vectors. Its two metrics reproduce cv2.FaceRecognizerSF.match exactly (to within 1e-5).\n\nPreview 'aligned' when a match looks wrong: a bad alignment is the usual cause. The quoted thresholds (cosine 0.363 / L2 1.128) are SFace's official operating points - remember to change 'threshold' when you switch metric.\n\nThe verdict is READ off 'CV Chart Series', not off a picture of the score array: with a one-person gallery the scores are (faces x 1), which 'Preview CV Array' would draw as a handful of pixels AND min-max normalize - and normalizing is exactly what destroys the absolute scale that makes 0.363 mean something. The chart pins the axis to 0..1, prints every value, draws the threshold as a line and splits the bars into two colours across it. Bar i is crop i of the 'aligned' preview, in the same order; the names come from one 'Input Text' list, the same way workflow 12 labels its image sheet."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
